(>> 6.1 Academic Phrasal Verbs in Scientific Discourse) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(A phrasal verb consists of a base verb combined with a preposition or adverbial) Tj
T*
(particle, creating an idiomatic meaning distinct from its component words. In formal) Tj
T*
(academic writing, single-word formal Latinate verbs are frequently preferred over) Tj
T*
(informal phrasal verbs.) Tj
T*
(* Common Academic Phrasal Verbs and Formal Latinate Equivalents:) Tj
T*
(- Break down: Decompose, disintegrate, or fail mechanically. \(Formal: Decompose /) Tj
T*
(Degrade\).) Tj
T*
(- Carry out: Execute or perform an experiment. \(Formal: Conduct / Execute /) Tj
T*
(Perform\).) Tj
T*
(- Figure out: Solve or comprehend a complex problem. \(Formal: Deduce / Determine /) Tj
T*
(Resolve\).) Tj
T*
(- Look into: Investigate or examine thoroughly. \(Formal: Investigate / Examine\).) Tj
T*
(- Put off: Postpone or delay an activity. \(Formal: Defer / Postpone\).) Tj
T*
(- Set up: Assemble apparatus or organize an assay. \(Formal: Assemble / Establish /) Tj
T*
(Configure\).) Tj
T*
(- Point out: Draw attention to a specific detail. \(Formal: Indicate / Highlight\).) Tj
T*
(- Bring about: Cause something to happen. \(Formal: Induce / Cause / Precipitate\).) Tj
T*
(- Phase out: Gradually discontinue use. \(Formal: Eliminate / Discontinue\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 6.2 Commonly Confused Words \(Homophones and Paronyms\)) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Affect vs. Effect:) Tj
T*
(- Affect \(Verb\): To influence or alter: "Substrate concentration affects enzymatic) Tj
T*
(velocity.") Tj
T*
(- Effect \(Noun\): The outcome or result: "The Pasteur effect describes glycolytic) Tj
T*
(inhibition by oxygen." \(Exception: Effect as verb means 'to bring about': effect) Tj
T*
(change\).) Tj
T*
(* Complement vs. Compliment:) Tj
T*
(- Complement: That which completes, enhances, or pairs harmoniously: "The two) Tj
T*
(primers complement the target DNA sequence.") Tj
T*
(- Compliment: An expression of praise or admiration: "The reviewer paid a generous) Tj
T*
(compliment.") Tj
T*
(* Principal vs. Principle:) Tj
T*
(- Principal: Primary, foremost, or chief executive of an institution: "The) Tj
T*
(principal investigator secured the grant.") Tj
T*
(- Principle: A fundamental scientific law, doctrine, or moral rule: "Le) Tj
T*
(Chatelier's principle predicts equilibrium shifts.") Tj
T*
(* Discrete vs. Discreet:) Tj
T*
(- Discrete: Distinct, separate, discontinuous: "The data points fall into four) Tj
T*
