(* Q2: Explain the stylistic differences between formal academic English and informal) Tj
T*
(conversational English regarding phrasal verbs, contractions, and passive structures.) Tj
T*
(Answer: \(1\) Phrasal Verbs vs. Latinate Vocabulary: Conversational English relies heavily) Tj
T*
(on multi-word phrasal verbs \(carry out, set up, figure out\). Formal academic English) Tj
T*
(replaces them with single-word precise Latinate verbs \(conduct, assemble, elucidate\) to) Tj
T*
(enhance lexical density and avoid ambiguous idiomatic interpretations. \(2\) Contractions:) Tj
T*
(Conversational English routinely contracts auxiliary verbs and negations \(don't, can't,) Tj
T*
(it's\). Formal academic writing strictly forbids contractions, requiring full spelled-out) Tj
T*
(forms \('do not', 'cannot', 'it is'\) to preserve structural formality. \(3\) Personal) Tj
T*
(Pronouns vs. Passive Voice: Conversational English relies on subjective pronouns \('We) Tj
T*
(did this', 'I observed that'\). Formal scientific English uses passive constructions and) Tj
T*
(impersonal nominalizations \('The assay was performed', 'Analysis revealed'\) to maintain) Tj
T*
(objective, reader-independent neutrality.) Tj
